= Distributional semantics — Simple =

'''Distributional semantics''' is a way to study meaning by looking at how language is used.

Words that appear in similar surroundings often have related meanings or roles.

A computer can count which words occur near each other and turn those patterns into numbers called vectors. Modern language models learn much more complicated versions of these representations.

This can help computers compare words, search documents, group similar expressions, and understand which meaning of a word fits a sentence.

But appearing in similar contexts does not prove that two words mean the same thing.

A distributional model learns patterns from language data. It does not automatically know what a word refers to in the real world or whether a statement is true.

